Which paths it covers [S]. The fold key uses only op2 & 0x3ff (lj_opt_fold.c:2650), so the rule matches every mode. That covers INDEX (lj_opt_narrow.c:456, from rec_idx_key at lj_record.c:1461, and select at lj_record.c:2049), CHECK (narrowing leaves), and ANY (lj_opt_narrow_toint at lj_opt_narrow.c:471: string.byte, string.sub and other library arguments). The table index takes no TOBIT path. TOBIT(CONV num.i64) is left alone. It could fold for width <= 52, but it is not needed here.

Why the bound is 32. oclj_bitwidth(y) = w means y is in [-2^(w-1), 2^(w-1)) on every execution. With w <= 32, y is in [-2^31, 2^31), so cvtsi2sd gives exactly d = y. The old conversion, asm_tointg at lj_asm_x86.h:830-842 (cvttsd2si r32, cvtsi2sd, ucomisd, jne/jp), then returns y, and its guard cannot fail; ANY mode's bare cvttsd2si also returns y. The new conversion (lj_asm_x86.h:972-977, `mov r32, r32`) yields y's low 32 bits, which as an int32 are y itself. So the value is the same and no exit is lost. The instruction is replaced before it is emitted, so no snapshot or other instruction refers to the dropped guard.

The bound is tight. At w = 33, y = 2^31 makes the old guard exit, while truncation gives -2^31. And y = -2^32+5, from `x ~>> 31` with x = -(2^32-5)*2^31, truncates to 5, a valid array index. The <= 33 sabotage build shows exactly these differences in t_idx.lua.

PHIs. When fleft is PHI-marked, the fold would put a new loop-body instruction on fleft->op1, a pre-roll reference that need not be PHI-marked. That case is excluded. The upstream CONV hint, lj_opt_loop.c:361, would also PHI-mark it, but I chose not to rely on that. A non-PHI fleft in the loop body has op1 that is either a loop instruction or an invariant or PHI-marked pre-roll reference, which is safe to use. The PHI marks are reliable at fold time: loop_unroll marks a reference (lj_opt_loop.c:334-341) when an earlier copy resolves to it, which happens before any later copy's fold runs.

What it inherits. The pre-roll's decision is replayed in the loop copy, and oclj_bitwidth recurses into PHI-marked operands. Both depend on the same per-iteration invariant as the prototype's width fold (design §3.5), and nothing beyond it.
